          <p><strong>Struggling with sleep?</strong> BetterHelp connects you with a licensed therapist from $60/week — including Australian-registered practitioners. <a href="https://partners.betterhelp.com/go/referral/carevues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get matched today &rarr;</a></p>
        </div>

        <p>Smart mattresses are having a moment. Eight Sleep — the company behind the Pod Pro — sits at the centre of that conversation, promising to actively improve your sleep through temperature control, sleep tracking, and adaptive algorithms. At several thousand dollars, it's not a small investment.</p>

        <p>We spent time testing the Eight Sleep Pod Pro as an Australian customer — ordering, setting up, and sleeping on it for several weeks. Here's what we found, unfiltered.</p>

        <h2>What Is Eight Sleep?</h2>

        <p>Eight Sleep is a New York-based sleep technology company that makes smart mattresses with built-in sensors, temperature control, and sleep tracking. Unlike a regular mattress you buy once and forget about, an Eight Sleep mattress is designed to actively respond to your body — cooling or heating through the night based on your sleep patterns and preferences.</p>

        <p>The current flagship is the <strong>Pod Pro</strong> — a dual-sided mattress (you and your partner can each set your own temperature zone) with an active grid layer that adjusts in real time. There's also the <strong>Pod Air</strong>, a lighter version without the active grid.</p>

        <h2>Key Features</h2>

        <h3>Temperature Control (Hot and Cold)</h3>
        <p>The headline feature is the ability to cool or heat your side of the bed independently. If you run hot, you can set it to cool; if you run cold, you can warm up. The range is roughly 13–42°C on the cool side and up to 38°C on the warm side.</p>

        <p>Users report that this alone makes a significant difference — particularly for people who share a bed with a partner who has different temperature preferences. No more fighting over the doona.</p>

        <h3>Sleep Tracking</h3>
        <p>Embedded sensors track your heart rate, HRV, respiratory rate, and movement throughout the night. This data feeds into an app that gives you a daily "Sleep Fitness" score — similar to how Oura or Whoop work, but in the mattress itself.</p>

        <p>Over time, the system learns your patterns and can automatically adjust temperature to help you fall asleep faster, stay in deeper deep sleep, and wake up at a optimal point in your sleep cycle via a gentle alarm.</p>

        <h3>Active Grid (Pod Pro only)</h3>
        <p>The Pod Pro includes an "Active Grid" — an algorithmic layer that subtly shifts the surface beneath you based on detected sleep stages. The company claims this reduces pressure points and can improve sleep quality. It's the main differentiator from the Pod Air.</p>

        <h3>Smart Alarm</h3>
        <p>The smart alarm wakes you at the optimal point in your sleep cycle — within a set window you define. Rather than a fixed alarm time, it finds the moment you're lightest asleep and nudges you then. Users report this makes mornings significantly less brutal.</p>

        <h2>Australian Pricing — What It Actually Costs</h2>

        <p>This is where it gets tricky for Australians. Eight Sleep is a US company, and their pricing is in USD. As of mid-2026:</p>

