        </table>

        <p>The key differentiator for Eight Sleep is the combination of temperature control + sleep tracking in one device. If you already have a mattress you like, adding an Oura Ring (~$449) gives you the tracking without the temperature piece. If you want both in one and have the budget, Eight Sleep is the only game in town.</p>

        <h2>What Australians Should Know Before Buying</h2>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Warranty and returns:</strong> Eight Sleep offers a 30-night trial period, but returns from Australia involve international shipping — it can be complicated and costly. Make sure you're confident before committing.</li>
          <li><strong>Voltage and compatibility:</strong> The Pod system uses US voltage (120V). You'll need a voltage converter for the power adapter in Australia, or Eight Sleep may ship a compatible unit — confirm this with their support before ordering.</li>
          <li><strong>Delivery lead times:</strong> Shipping from the US to Australia typically takes 2–4 weeks for the mattress itself, plus additional time for the Pod hub (the climate control unit that sits beside the bed).</li>
          <li><strong>Customer support:</strong> Eight Sleep's support team is US-based. For Australian customers, this means email/chat only — no local phone support. Response times are generally good but timezone differences apply.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Who Is It For?</h2>

        <p>Eight Sleep makes sense in a specific set of circumstances:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>You share a bed</strong> with a partner who has very different temperature needs, and dual-zone temperature control would genuinely improve both your sleep.</li>
          <li><strong>You've tried the basics</strong> (good mattress, blackout curtains, limiting screen time) and still aren't sleeping well, and you want a system that actively tries to improve your sleep rather than just measuring it.</li>
          <li><strong>You value data</strong> and want to track your sleep over time with the kind of granularity that Eight Sleep offers.</li>
          <li><strong>You have the budget</strong> to spend $4,000–5,000 on a sleep system and you're confident the investment is worth it for your situation.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>It makes less sense if:</p>

        <ul>
          <li>You're on a tight budget — the cost is significant and there are effective alternatives (like an Oura Ring + mattress topper) for a fraction of the price.</li>
          <li>You sleep alone and don't particularly struggle with temperature — you might not get the full value of the dual-zone system.</li>
          <li>You already have a high-quality mattress you're happy with — the sleep tracking alone might not justify the upgrade cost.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Our Verdict</h2>

        <p>The Eight Sleep Pod Pro is an impressive piece of technology. The temperature control is genuinely useful — particularly for couples — and the sleep tracking is comprehensive. If you're someone who has tried the obvious sleep hygiene improvements and still isn't sleeping well, Eight Sleep is one of the most sophisticated consumer tools available for addressing the problem actively rather than just monitoring it.</p>

        <p>But the cost is real. For most Australians, the total landed cost of $4,000–5,000 AUD is a significant investment. There are cheaper ways to get temperature-regulated sleep (some premium mattress brands offer cooling layers) and there are better ways to get comprehensive sleep tracking (like the Oura Ring at $449) — though neither combination hits everything Eight Sleep offers in one device.</p>

        <p>If you've done the research, you're confident the dual-zone temperature control would meaningfully improve your sleep, and the budget makes sense for your situation — it's a well-built product that delivers on its promises. Just make sure you understand the international purchase realities before committing.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <details>
            <summary>Does Eight Sleep ship to Australia?</summary>
            <p>Yes. Eight Sleep ships internationally to Australia. However, shipping costs are significant and import duties may apply on electronics of this value. The total landed cost for a Queen Pod Pro is typically $4,000–5,000 AUD.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Do I need a subscription to use Eight Sleep?</summary>
            <p>No — the core temperature control and basic sleep tracking work without a subscription. However, the Essentials+ (~$19/month) or Premium (~$29/month) plans unlock historical data, advanced insights, smart alarm features, and integration with other health platforms. Without a subscription, you get less of the data-driven value.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can I use Eight Sleep with different voltage in Australia?</summary>
            <p>The Pod system uses US voltage. You may need a voltage converter for the power adapter, or Eight Sleep may provide an AU-compatible unit — confirm this with their support team before ordering to avoid damaging the device.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How long does Eight Sleep last?</summary>
            <p>Eight Sleep says the Pod Pro mattress is designed to last 10+ years with normal use. The cover is removable and washable. The electronics hub (the Pod) has a typical hardware warranty of 2 years. Based on user reports, the build quality is solid — but at this price point, the long-term reliability is important to consider.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Is Eight Sleep better than an Oura Ring?</summary>
            <p>They're different tools. The Oura Ring (~$449 AUD) gives you best-in-class sleep tracking in a wearable — HRV, sleep stages, resting heart rate, and temperature variation. Eight Sleep gives you all that tracking <em>plus</em> active temperature control in a mattress. If you want both sleep tracking and temperature control in one device, Eight Sleep is the better choice. If you just want tracking, the Oura is significantly cheaper and more proven in the Australian market.</p>
          </details>
        </div>

